{
  "image_name": "57af821554fef32190c2beedfe831660.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts three intuitive pictures of electron transport and then shows how these lead to different current-flow patterns in confined devices. At the top, electron motion is compared to pinball, billiards, and fluid flow. In the pinball-like case, electrons mainly scatter off impurities and emit or absorb phonons. In the billiard-like case, boundary collisions dominate, with electrons reflecting from device walls. In the fluid-dynamics case, frequent electron–electron collisions make the carriers behave collectively like a viscous fluid, while momentum can still relax through phonons.\n\nThe lower part illustrates how these transport regimes change current profiles in several geometries. In a simple channel, the hydrodynamic regime produces a curved, Poiseuille-like velocity profile, unlike the flatter Ohmic case. In a constriction, the two regimes give visibly different streamline shapes near the narrow opening. In the “vicinity” and “double chamber” geometries, the hydrodynamic flow forms vortices or backflow beside the main current path, whereas the Ohmic regime spreads more directly and diffusively. Overall, the figure emphasizes that when electron–electron scattering dominates, electrical transport can resemble classical fluid motion, producing nontrivial streamlines and whirlpools not expected in ordinary diffusive conduction.",
